WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.690 --> 00:00:05.310
Some of animation's most memorable seem like
real people, partly because their appearance

00:00:05.310 --> 00:00:08.240
and personalities were actually inspired by
real people.

00:00:08.440 --> 00:00:13.700
Here are a few memorable examples of beloved
cartoon characters and their real-life counterparts.

00:00:14.140 --> 00:00:15.720
Mr. Burns - The Simpsons

00:00:16.020 --> 00:00:20.240
It took a little bit of a lot of fearsome
individuals to add up to the most evil man

00:00:20.240 --> 00:00:20.880
in Springfield.

00:00:21.100 --> 00:00:25.360
Simpsons creator Matt Groening says he got
Burns' demeanor and some of his appearance

00:00:25.370 --> 00:00:27.930
from David Bailey, his high school math teacher.

00:00:27.930 --> 00:00:29.450
"Am I a capitalist?

00:00:29.450 --> 00:00:31.160
Do I believe in the free enterprise system?

00:00:31.360 --> 00:00:31.860
Yes.

00:00:32.540 --> 00:00:33.040
Check.

00:00:34.100 --> 00:00:34.600
Am I greedy?

00:00:34.720 --> 00:00:35.360
No.

00:00:35.360 --> 00:00:36.560
I think I'm a very generous person."

00:00:36.820 --> 00:00:41.039
Then he added in a little bit of Standard
Oil tycoon John D. Rockefeller so he'd appear

00:00:41.040 --> 00:00:44.170
more like, quote, "the embodiment of corporate
greed."

00:00:44.700 --> 00:00:45.220
"Family.

00:00:45.740 --> 00:00:46.240
Religion.

00:00:46.700 --> 00:00:47.280
Friendship.

00:00:47.860 --> 00:00:51.500
These are the three demons you must slay if
you wish to succeed in business."

00:00:51.800 --> 00:00:56.460
When it came time to render him in cartoon
form, animator David Silverman took other

00:00:56.460 --> 00:01:01.179
inspirations, including the body of a praying
mantis and the head and face of Barry Diller

00:01:01.180 --> 00:01:03.140
— the founder of the Fox network.

00:01:03.760 --> 00:01:04.500
Bugs Bunny

00:01:04.800 --> 00:01:09.280
The reason Bugs Bunny eats carrots has more
to do with classic Hollywood movie star Clark

00:01:09.280 --> 00:01:12.410
Gable than the dietary preferences of real
rabbits.

00:01:12.410 --> 00:01:16.880
Probably best known today for his role as
Rhett Butler in 1939's Gone With the Wind,

00:01:17.040 --> 00:01:22.460
Gable won the Academy Award for Best Actor
for the 1934 comedy It Happened One Night,

00:01:22.460 --> 00:01:25.880
portraying a sarcastic and scrappy unemployed
newspaper reporter.

00:01:26.040 --> 00:01:30.160
In one scene, he discusses his hitchhiking
skills — while munching on a carrot…

00:01:30.680 --> 00:01:32.060
"It's all a matter of knowing how to handle
it."

00:01:32.800 --> 00:01:34.440
"You're an expert, I suppose?"

00:01:34.900 --> 00:01:35.400
"Expert?

00:01:35.400 --> 00:01:36.880
I'm gonna write a book about it."

00:01:37.220 --> 00:01:42.300
Animator Friz Freleng said he lifted Gable's
behavior in this scene when creating the sarcastic

00:01:42.300 --> 00:01:43.540
and scrappy Bugs …

00:01:45.960 --> 00:01:46.680
"I live here.

00:01:46.920 --> 00:01:48.880
It's my home, such as it is."

00:01:53.760 --> 00:01:55.400
Rocko - Rocko's Modern Life

00:01:55.660 --> 00:01:59.980
Rocko is anxious, overwhelmed, and out of
step with the world around him — like the

00:01:59.990 --> 00:02:04.870
stock male character in a number of Woody
Allen movies, often played by Allen himself.

00:02:04.870 --> 00:02:09.250
In his pitch to Nickelodeon, Rocko's Modern
Life creator Joe Murray described his title

00:02:09.250 --> 00:02:13.660
character — a wallaby with an Australian
accent — as "a young, anthropomorphic Woody

00:02:13.660 --> 00:02:17.620
Allen, who has just moved away from home into
a surrealistic adult world."

00:02:18.040 --> 00:02:19.500
Troy McClure - The Simpsons

00:02:19.720 --> 00:02:24.240
You might remember him from The Simpsons as
an actor and heartthrob well past his prime,

00:02:24.240 --> 00:02:28.060
who can only get work in B-movies, educational
films, and infomercials.

00:02:28.460 --> 00:02:31.380
"A few weeks ago, I was a washed up actor
with a drinking problem.

00:02:31.700 --> 00:02:36.320
Then Brad Goodman came along and gave me this
job and a can of fortified wine."

00:02:37.020 --> 00:02:42.040
His name combines two hunky actors who've
been largely forgotten: Troy Donahue and Doug

00:02:42.040 --> 00:02:42.540
McClure.

00:02:42.640 --> 00:02:47.800
Simpsons writer Mike Reiss revealed on a Simpsons
DVD commentary track that he met Doug McClure's

00:02:47.800 --> 00:02:51.360
daughter, who told him her dad figured out
they were making fun of him…and that his

00:02:51.360 --> 00:02:53.480
kids called him "Troy" when he wasn't in the
room.

00:02:53.720 --> 00:02:54.600
That's brutal...

00:02:55.040 --> 00:02:56.380
Chuckie - Rugrats

00:02:56.510 --> 00:03:00.440
While he still might be best known as the
frontman of forward-thinking rock band Devo,

00:03:00.440 --> 00:03:05.340
Mark Mothersbaugh is now one of the most sought
after film composers working in movies and

00:03:05.340 --> 00:03:05.840
TV.

00:03:05.920 --> 00:03:09.680
Not only did he create Rugrats' memorably
weird synth and vocal-based music,

00:03:16.260 --> 00:03:19.260
...but his 
unique personal style also inspired the design

00:03:19.260 --> 00:03:21.620
of Chuckie, one of the show's lead characters.

00:03:22.020 --> 00:03:23.640
Ursula - The Little Mermaid

00:03:24.060 --> 00:03:28.930
The villainous sea witch in the 1989 Disney
classic was inspired by Glenn Milstead, better

00:03:28.930 --> 00:03:32.060
known as Divine, star of several John Waters
films.

00:03:32.260 --> 00:03:36.800
Little Mermaid lyricist and producer Howard
Ashman knew Divine, having once frequented

00:03:36.819 --> 00:03:39.020
the same underground club scene in Baltimore.

00:03:39.020 --> 00:03:43.819
Ashman worked with Disney artists on preliminary
sketches to add in a shock of hair, a ton

00:03:43.820 --> 00:03:48.600
of eyeshadow, and other features that made
Ursula look almost exactly like Divine.

00:03:48.920 --> 00:03:50.560
Krusty the Clown - The Simpsons

00:03:50.860 --> 00:03:55.340
In the early days of television, a guy named
James Allen, better known as Rusty Nails,

00:03:55.349 --> 00:03:58.989
had a show on local Portland TV that aired
on Saturday mornings.

00:03:58.989 --> 00:04:03.349
Simpsons creator Matt Groening grew up in
Portland and watched the show as a kid, and

00:04:03.349 --> 00:04:08.220
created Krusty the Clown in honor of Rusty,
although he says Rusty wasn't nearly as surly

00:04:08.220 --> 00:04:09.220
and disenchanted.

00:04:10.120 --> 00:04:13.340
"I could pull a better cartoon out of my … hey!

00:04:13.340 --> 00:04:13.840
Whoa!

00:04:14.100 --> 00:04:15.360
Wasn't that great, kids?"

00:04:15.640 --> 00:04:17.160
Butters - South Park

00:04:17.460 --> 00:04:21.970
Eric Stough has been helping make South Park
since the early 1990s, when it was just a

00:04:21.970 --> 00:04:24.660
couple of crudely animated Christmas-themed
shorts.

00:04:24.660 --> 00:04:28.680
Today, Stough still works as the animation
director for the long-running series.

00:04:28.680 --> 00:04:32.860
Another indelible contribution to the show:
He's the inspiration for the sweet, naïve,

00:04:32.860 --> 00:04:35.130
innocent, and incredibly unlucky Butters.

00:04:35.130 --> 00:04:39.620
Stough says he's almost as agreeable as Butters,
and had a similar strict upbringing.

00:04:39.620 --> 00:04:43.970
The other South Park guys once nicknamed him
Little Brother, which evolved into Little

00:04:43.970 --> 00:04:46.120
Buddy, then Budders…then Butters.

00:04:46.520 --> 00:04:48.480
"Kim Kardashian is so sexy.

00:04:48.700 --> 00:04:50.900
Her butt is like a big mountain of pudding."

00:04:51.260 --> 00:04:52.260
Thanks for watching!

00:04:52.500 --> 00:04:55.270
